1: # Relative old-u / new-v source-zero comparison
2: 
3: Proved punctured-family comparison after root audit, 17 November 2026. The finite
4: fan execution is separate. This note retains the original normalized
5: E7 presentation, the literal new presentation and their cap/annular
6: arrows. Sections RCZ.5–7 explicitly complete the previously unselected
7: reference annular marking F0, starting from the entire old map at one
8: marked parameter. The earlier unresolved version is preserved in
9: records under `pre_reference_F0_...md`. No numerical source-loop
10: return is assigned by this comparison.
11: 
12: ## RCZ.1. The actual common source, line and suspension
13: 
14: The coordinate arrow is
15: \[
16:  I_u:(x,y,t,u)\longmapsto
17:  (X,Y,T,v)=(u^2x,u^3y,u^4t,u^3).
18: \]
19: It carries G and O to their marked counterparts and carries the
20: canonical Cartier line \(M_a=\mathcal O(aG-O)\), for
21: \((a,b)=(1,2),(2,1)\), by its actual divisor map. The primitive
22: translation lift and selected-base coordinate satisfy
23: \[
24:  I_uU_{a,\mathrm{old}}I_u^{-1}=u^{2a}U_{a,\mathrm{new}},
25:  \qquad q_{\mathrm{old}}=u^{-4}q_{\mathrm{new}}.
26: \]
27: Since \(ab=2\), these factors cancel in the actual suspension
28: \(2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda qU_a^b\). The scalar is therefore the same
29: literal scalar, not a scalar with an omitted u-power.
30: 
31: The cusp Tate period and marked section point agree under I_u.
32: Its physical-height unit is retained as
33: \(q_{\mathrm{old}}/q_{\mathrm{new}}=u^{-4}\). On the u-annulus
34: this is an invertible integer-power toric multiplier. It preserves
35: the chosen fan and physical delta character. Its parameter winding
36: is retained; no global logarithm of u is chosen here.
37: 
38: For a cap of order m, the root arrow on the logarithmic u cover is
39: \[
40:  z_{\mathrm{old}}=u^{4/m}z_{\mathrm{new}}.
41: \]
42: For the good root \(m=4p\), the minimal coordinates satisfy
43: \(X'_{\mathrm{new}}=x'_{\mathrm{old}}\) and
44: \(Y'_{\mathrm{new}}=y'_{\mathrm{old}}\). For the reduced root
45: \(m=4p-1\), they satisfy
46: \[
47:  X'_{\mathrm{new}}=u^{-2/m}x'_{\mathrm{old}},\qquad
48:  Y'_{\mathrm{new}}=u^{-3/m}y'_{\mathrm{old}}.
49: \]
50: These maps carry the actual section to
51: \((0,vz_{\mathrm{new}}^{3p})\), preserve the specified resolution
52: ideals, and commute with the root deck D. Their full positive
53: u-loop endpoint is \(D_{\mathrm{new}}^{-4}\). In the reduced
54: coordinates this is exactly
55: \[
56:  (z,X',Y')\longmapsto
57:  (\zeta_m^{-4}z,\zeta_m^{-2}X',\zeta_m^{-3}Y'),
58: \]
59: because \(4p=m+1\). This is the root endpoint; it is not silently
60: replaced by the identity on the affine cap.
61: 
62: ## RCZ.2. The extending fourth translation and its retained period
63: 
64: Let R be the actual division section \(mR=Q\), with the branch
65: specified in F41 or F37. The signed affine cap is represented by
66: \(A=L D\), where L covers \(-nR\), and its scalar normalization
67: is the one giving \(A^m=B^n\). We retain the finite eigenspace
68: construction used for L.
69: 
70: Its central commutator character is one in both actual branches.
71: In the good branch R specializes to O, so L on the central compact
72: elliptic fibre is a scalar line automorphism. Such a scalar commutes
73: with the actual D lift. In the reduced branch the line is
74: \(N=\mathcal O(2G'-O)\). The doubling formula puts 2G' at O on
75: the reduced central fibre. Both meet its smooth locus, so the
76: restriction of this Cartier line is literally the trivial line;
77: its component degrees are also zero. In a trivialization of that
78: line, the central line multipliers of D and L are holomorphic
79: units on a compact connected reduced curve, hence constants.
80: Their source maps commute because D fixes R. Their line maps
81: therefore commute as well. Thus the finite eigencharacter is one.
82: 
83: The actual finite eigenspace lift can consequently be chosen with
84: \(DLD^{-1}=L\). Its norm normalization is a base-invariant unit
85: and preserves this equality. This is the exact normalized lift
86: used in the cap; any initial unit discrepancy is removed by its
87: finite averaging conjugation, with that conjugation retained on
88: the annulus. In these charts
89: \[
90:                          A^4D^{-4}=L^4.
91: \]
92: The map on the right is an actual extending line map. In the good
93: branch its central source translation is zero. In the reduced
94: branch R specializes to the nonidentity two-torsion point, so
95: \(-4nR\) also specializes to zero. Hence L^4 restricts to a
96: nonzero scalar on the central fibre. On a simply connected
97: parameter chart divide by that scalar, take its scalar logarithm,
98: and use the inverse of the exponential near the identity of the
99: extending translation group. This produces a small extending Lie
100: logarithm of L^4. It does not identify that logarithm with four
101: times an annular logarithm of L.
102: 
103: In particular, on the marked good fourth root of the reduced
104: branch, write its square lattice as \(\mathbb Z+i\mathbb Z\),
105: with \(R(0)=(1+i)/2\). A logarithm tending to this half-period
106: satisfies
107: \[
108:              4\log R-\log(4R)=2(1+i),
109: \]
110: where the second logarithm is the small one. With signed n the
111: source part is multiplied by n. The full line-period vector is
112: the actual difference of the two lifted line logarithms; it
113: includes the Cartier factor of automorphy. It is retained as an
114: integral annular cochain. No parameter-dependent multiple of
115: this source period is asserted to extend across the reduced cap.
116: 
117: The exact finite-unit conjugation has a useful elementary form.
118: In the input-scalar convention, let an actual cap map be
119: \(A'=\beta A\), where \(\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}\beta(D^jz)=1\)
120: and its central character has already been matched. Put
121: \(g=\log\beta\), with central value zero, and
122: \[
123:  f(z)=-\frac1m\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}j\,g(D^jz).
124: \]
125: Then \(f(Dz)-f(z)=-g(z)\), and multiplication by \(e^f\)
126: conjugates A' to A. The maps and logarithms depend holomorphically
127: on the parameter on each chosen parameter chart. The finite
128: character is matched before this zero-central logarithm is taken.
129: 
130: ## RCZ.3. The relative Lie solve and the full groupoid
131: 
132: Over a simply connected small parameter chart in the u-annulus,
133: the actual extending translation Lie bundle has
134: \[
135:  0\longrightarrow\mathcal O\delta\longrightarrow V
136:        \longrightarrow\mathcal O(-1)\longrightarrow0
137: \]
138: on the coarse source base. This is the relative version of CP.1.
139: Indeed the root substitution sends the source dualizing vector
140: to \(z^k\) times the root vector, with its exact deck character
141: \(\zeta_m^k\), and \(0\le k<m\). Its invariant extension is
142: therefore the original source Lie line. The Weierstrass invariant
143: differential and I_u supply the actual relative line frames;
144: their extra u-powers are units on this parameter chart.
145: 
146: The extension splits by the ordinary Cauchy/Laurent solution for
147: \(\mathcal O(1)\). The same fixed-circle integrals solve every
148: V-valued base one-cocycle for \(\mathcal O\) and
149: \(\mathcal O(-1)\). These integrals preserve holomorphic
150: parameter dependence. Finite averaging applies to these complex
151: vector cochains. Thus the relative first coherent cohomology of
152: V over the source base is zero, with an explicit parameter-dependent
153: solution operator.
154: 
155: The exponential sequence is used on the entire root atlas and
156: its groupoid,
157: \[
158:  0\longrightarrow H\longrightarrow V\longrightarrow A
159:        \longrightarrow0.
160: \]
161: At a reduced root, the actual suspension-power removal of its
162: component translation is made before taking the identity-component
163: logarithm, exactly as in CP.3. Lie exponentiation alone is not used
164: to erase a disconnected component.
165: Atlas logarithms are not presumed invariant. Their ordinary
166: overlap discrepancies and all finite cyclic-group discrepancies
167: form a total integral two-cocycle. Its finite terms retain the
168: literal equation \(A^m=B^n\), the central line character and
169: the actual period lift. The doubled period in RCZ.2 is carried
170: by that total cochain whenever parameter charts are changed.
171: Only after the two actual total two-cocycles have been compared
172: is the remaining V-valued one-cocycle solved by the preceding
173: operator. No assertion about surjectivity of exponentiation on
174: coarse invariant sections is used.
175: 
176: All resulting local exponentials are the extending source/line
177: maps of CP.3. At the cusp their two characters become bounded
178: holomorphic units after the actual integral component reindexing;
179: at every finite root they are the line lifts on the actual
180: resolved source, with the stated finite character. These local
181: maps therefore transport the annular and Cox normal charts as
182: actual maps.
183: 
184: ## RCZ.4. The parameter scalar group and explicit analytic factorization
185: 
186: On the punctured source-parameter annulus every source has the
187: same marked Mordell--Weil group \(\mathbb ZG\). The endpoint
188: calculation CP.3–4 applies relative to this annulus. In particular
189: an actual global extending translation lifts off the selected cusp,
190: its source part is \(jG\) with j locally constant, and its line
191: part is a rational base function times the primitive lift U_G^j.
192: The cap orders and root characters give its exact valuations.
193: The selected cusp uses its actual integer reindexing. Taking the
194: degree of that rational divisor gives the same equality
195: \(j=bk\) as CP.4, and the norm ratio identifies the full map
196: with a physical scalar. Evaluation at one ordinary marked origin
197: shows that this scalar is holomorphic in the source parameter.
198: Conversely every such holomorphic nonzero scalar extends through
199: all the actual charts. The relative global translation group is
200: therefore the physical \(\mathcal O^*\), with no omitted finite
201: kernel.
202: 
203: Here is the explicit parameter factorization used for locally
204: constructed family comparisons. Cover a slightly larger round
205: u-annulus by two angular charts U_+,U_-, with simply connected
206: overlap components. Write the actual scalar transition as c and
207: choose a holomorphic logarithm \(\ell\) on each overlap
208: component. Choose a smooth angular partition \(\chi_++\chi_-=1\)
209: subordinate to these charts, and put
210: \[
211:  b_+=\chi_-\ell,\qquad b_-=-\chi_+\ell.
212: \]
213: These are extended by zero away from the respective overlap
214: supports. They satisfy \(b_+-b_-=\ell\), and
215: \(\bar\partial b_+=\bar\partial b_-\) is a globally defined
216: smooth (0,1)-form \(\eta=f(u)d\bar u\). Choose a radial cutoff
217: equal to one on a smaller closed annulus carrying the loop and
218: compactly supported in the larger annulus. Extend the cut-off
219: coefficient by zero to the plane and set
220: \[
221:  v(u)=\frac1\pi\int_{\mathbb C}
222:              \frac{\widetilde f(w)}{u-w}\,dA(w).
223: \]
224: The distribution identity
225: \(\bar\partial_u(1/(\pi(u-w)))=\delta_w\) gives
226: \(\bar\partial v=\eta\) on the smaller annulus. Thus
227: \[
228:  h_+=e^{b_+-v},\qquad h_-=e^{b_--v}
229: \]
230: are nonvanishing holomorphic functions with \(h_+/h_-=c\).
231: When the local comparison maps have the actual arrow
232: \(I_+=cI_-\), the maps \(h_+^{-1}I_+\) and
233: \(h_-^{-1}I_-\) glue literally. All multipliers are physical
234: scalars; their action on the line and normal coordinates is the
235: specified one. This gives the analytic factorization, including
236: both overlap components and any winding of c.
237: 
238: For the actual scalar rescaling \(\lambda=v^N\lambda_0\),
239: write \(N=km\), \(v=u^3\), and
240: \(t=\log u/(2\pi i)\). The old and new ordinary forward decks
241: are \(q_{\mathrm{old}}^{3kn}\) and
242: \(q_{\mathrm{new}}^{3kn}\); their ratio is
243: \(u^{-12kn}\). The literal compensating scalar cochain is
244: \[
245:  c_0(t)=\exp\bigl(2\pi i\,6kn\,t(t-1)\bigr),
246:  \qquad \frac{c_0(t+1)}{c_0(t)}=u^{12kn}.
247: \]
248: This is retained in the forward endpoint square. It is not
249: declared to be a single-valued multiplier by itself, or to extend
250: over u=0 in the old coordinates. Any remaining scalar transitions
251: are treated by the factorization just given.
252: 
253: ## RCZ.5. Completing the previously unselected annular marking
254: 
255: Section 21 of `part3_source_zero_completion_working.md` left F0
256: specified as a holomorphic actual reference-scalar overlap, without
257: choosing its absolute integral annular marking. We now make that
258: choice. Fix a nonzero u* and put v*=u*^3. Transport the **entire**
259: old ordinary-to-cap map at u* through the canonical source/Cartier
260: arrow I_{u*}, including its chosen root cut, signed norm, prescribed
261: \(d\delta\), and every period in its lifted logarithm. Call this
262: actual map F*. The new F0 is constructed below to restrict exactly
263: to F*. This is completion of the previously unselected data; it is
264: not a proof that an unrecorded different choice had the same integer.
265: The new analytic norm root uses the transported old value at this
266: reference point and the same prescribed finite character. Its
267: nonvanishing norm unit extends on the root polydisc, so this branch
268: continues analytically there. The finite unit conjugation in RCZ.2
269: is included before F* is regarded as a map into that new cap.
270: 
271: Choose a fixed cap-overlap annulus C in the T-base sufficiently far
272: from the colliding finite fibres. All source fibres above C remain
273: smooth elliptic curves for v in a full sufficiently small disc D,
274: including v=0. The root source, its actual corrected Cartier line,
275: its division branch and its normalized A0 extend on this product by
276: the displayed good/reduced equations and resolved line diagrams.
277: At a fixed sufficiently small reference scalar, the ordinary and
278: cap norm quotients are therefore smooth proper families of compact
279: complex tori over C×D.
280: 
281: There are literal local comparison maps on this whole product.
282: Cover C by two angular charts C_+,C_-. Choose a root branch z_i(q)
283: on each. Restriction to that chosen sheet gives a map from the
284: ordinary pullback line quotient to the cap quotient. It has no
285: residual quotient: an element \(A^jB^h\) fixing that sheet has
286: \(j=mk\), and is exactly \(B^{nk+h}\). Thus the fibre on the
287: chosen sheet is precisely \(N^\times/\langle B\rangle\).
288: The source/Cartier identification supplies the claimed local map.
289: On overlap, its map between sheets is the literal indicated power
290: of A0, including its line character and the relation \(A_0^m=B_0^n\).
291: This description continues through v=0 on C; no singular source
292: fibre occurs in this overlap annulus.
293: 
294: The two local comparisons differ by translations in the actual
295: Jacobian family J over C×D. Its Lie bundle V is holomorphically
296: trivializable there by the source dualizing vector, physical delta,
297: and the same additive Cauchy splitting of their extension. One can
298: also use the literal constant-factor-of-automorphy presentation of
299: the degree-zero elliptic line on each simply connected angular
300: chart. Its periods and marked Picard point are holomorphic in
301: (q,v), including at v=0. These are frames of the actual line
302: translation group, not an identification with an unrelated torus.
303: 
304: Correct each local comparison on C_i×{v*} to equal F*. The required
305: translation has a holomorphic logarithm on the simply connected
306: C_i. Extend its coefficients in the just specified Lie frame
307: holomorphically in v, and exponentiate. This makes local actual
308: maps E_i on C_i×D satisfying E_i|_{v*}=F*.
309: 
310: Their overlap translation is the identity at v*. It therefore has
311: a unique holomorphic Lie logarithm \(\ell_{ij}(q,v)\) whose value
312: at v* is zero. To construct it, lift along the simply connected
313: v-disc through the exponential covering of the smooth torus
314: family, starting at zero; uniqueness gives holomorphic dependence
315: on q as well. The overlap components are simply connected. Any
316: ordinary or finite-group discrepancy of these logarithms is an
317: integral period and is zero at v*. It remains zero throughout D.
318: The finite root groupoid has not been forgotten: its free-sheet
319: description above carries every A0 power and its norm to these
320: ordinary overlap maps. In particular its bar-resolution terms are
